{0 A first CLI}

This tutorial writes [check], a command that runs test suites. It shows
them as progress rows while they run, then prints a table of their results.
By the end, [check] has:

{ul
{- [--color], [--theme] and [--progress] options;}
{- plain lines on a pipe, or with [--progress=plain];}
{- a cram test whose output is the same on every machine.}}

Each section adds to the one before it.

{1:setup Setup}

[check] uses the [console] and [console-eio] packages, with [eio_main] to
run Eio, {{:https://erratique.ch/software/cmdliner}Cmdliner} for its options
and {{:https://erratique.ch/software/logs}Logs} for its messages:

{v
opam install console console-eio eio_main cmdliner logs
v}

Its [dune] file:

{v
(executable
 (public_name check)
 (libraries
  console console.tty console-eio
  eio eio_main fmt fmt.cli cmdliner logs))
v}

{1:options Colour and theme}

A program decides on colour once, before it prints. {!Console_tty.setup}
reads [NO_COLOR], [TERM], [COLORTERM] and the locale. From them it sets
colour, colour depth and UTF-8 on the standard outputs.

Given a style renderer, [setup] uses that one instead.
[Fmt_cli.style_renderer] reads one from a [--color=auto|always|never]
option.

A formatter also keeps a {{!Console.Theme}theme}. The table prints on
standard output and the progress rows on standard error, so [setup] gives
both outputs the theme a [--theme] option reads:

{@ocaml[
let setup color theme =
  Console_tty.setup ?style_renderer:color ~theme ();
  Logs.set_level (Some Logs.Info)

let theme =
  Cmdliner.Arg.(
    value
    & opt (enum Console.Theme.all) Console.Theme.default
    & info [ "theme" ] ~doc:"The look of the output.")

let options = Cmdliner.Term.(const setup $ Fmt_cli.style_renderer () $ theme)
]}

{!Console.Theme.all} lists the predefined themes under their names. These
are the values [--theme] accepts:

{@ocaml[
# List.map fst Console.Theme.all;;
- : string list =
["default"; "ascii"; "dos"; "matrix"; "rainbow"; "dracula"; "nord";
 "solarized-dark"; "solarized-light"; "gruvbox-dark"; "gruvbox-light";
 "catppuccin-latte"; "catppuccin-mocha"; "tokyo-night"; "one-dark";
 "monokai"; "github-dark"; "github-light"]
]}

{!Console.Theme.default} reads on any background. A theme named after a
colour scheme, such as [dracula], is for a terminal that uses that scheme.

{1:table A table}

A table is a list of columns and rows of {{!Console.Span}spans}. It is as
wide as its content needs, and no wider than the formatter's margin. Its
frame, header style and colours come from the theme of the formatter that
prints it:

{@ocaml[
# let suites = [ ("parser", 3); ("layout", 2) ];;
val suites : (string * int) list = [("parser", 3); ("layout", 2)]
# let results suites =
    let row (name, n) =
      [ Console.Span.text name; Console.Span.text (Int.to_string n) ]
    in
    Console.Table.(
      v
        [
          column (Console.Span.text "Suite");
          column ~align:`Right (Console.Span.text "Tests");
        ]
        (List.map row suites));;
val results : (string * int) list -> Console.Table.t = <fun>
# Fmt.pr "%a@." Console.Table.pp (results suites);;
╭────────┬───────╮
│ Suite  │ Tests │
├────────┼───────┤
│ parser │     3 │
│ layout │     2 │
╰────────┴───────╯
- : unit = ()
]}

{1:progress Progress rows}

{!Console_eio.run} opens a display on standard error. It passes the
display to a function and closes it when the function returns.

{!Console.Display.with_task} runs a function as a task. Its row shows while
the function runs. The task succeeds when the function returns, and fails
when it raises. {!Console.Display.set_count} says how much work is done:

{@ocaml[
let test display (name, total) =
  Console.Display.with_task display name @@ fun task ->
  for cur = 1 to total do
    Console.Display.set_count task ~cur ~total
  done;
  Logs.info (fun m -> m "%d tests passed" total)

let run_all ~clock ~progress suites =
  Console_eio.run ~clock ~progress ~header:"Testing" (fun display ->
      Eio.Fiber.List.iter (test display) suites)
]}

[Eio.Fiber.List.iter] runs each suite in a fiber of its own. Suites that
wait, for a child process for example, run side by side, one row each.

A task's output shows under its row while it runs, and stays if the task
fails. Its output is:

{ul
{- the [Logs] messages logged inside it, as here;}
{- the lines it prints with [Fmt.pr];}
{- the lines it hands to {!Console.Display.log}.}}

Outside a task, output goes above the rows.
{{!Console_eio.output}Where text goes} says more.

{1:program The whole program}

[check] sets up the outputs, runs the suites, then prints the table. A
[--progress] option says how the progress shows, as that of [docker build]
does:

{ul
{- [auto], the default, redraws the rows on a terminal;}
{- [tty] redraws them on any output;}
{- [plain] prints lines in turn, as on a pipe, even on a terminal;}
{- [quiet] prints only what fails;}
{- [json] writes one JSON object for each event
   ({{!Console_eio.json}JSON events}).}}

{!Console_eio.val-progress} names these values for the [progress] argument
of {!Console_eio.run}:

{@ocaml[
let progress =
  Cmdliner.Arg.(
    value
    & opt (enum Console_eio.progress) `Auto
    & info [ "progress" ]
        ~doc:
          "How progress shows: $(b,auto), $(b,tty), $(b,plain), $(b,quiet) or \
           $(b,json).")

let check () progress =
  Eio_main.run @@ fun env ->
  run_all ~clock:(Eio.Stdenv.mono_clock env) ~progress suites;
  Fmt.pr "%a@." Console.Table.pp (results suites)

let cmd =
  Cmdliner.Cmd.v (Cmdliner.Cmd.info "check")
    Cmdliner.Term.(const check $ options $ progress)

let main () = exit (Cmdliner.Cmd.eval cmd)
]}

Its entry point is [let () = main ()].

{1:pipe Output on a pipe}

When the output is not a terminal, as in a pipe or a CI log, nothing is
redrawn. Instead:

{ul
{- a task prints a line when it starts;}
{- each of its messages follows, after its name;}
{- it prints a line when it ends, with its count and its time, then
   [failed] or [cancelled] if it did not succeed;}
{- the header comes last, as a summary.}}

Without [--color=always], a pipe gets no colour. Here [check] runs with its
output captured and [CONSOLE_FROZEN_CLOCK] set, which the
{{!testing}next section} explains:

{@ocaml[
# Unix.putenv "CONSOLE_FROZEN_CLOCK" "1";;
- : unit = ()
# Cmdliner.Cmd.eval ~argv:[| "check"; "--theme=dos" |] cmd;;
[-] parser
  parser: 3 tests passed
[+] parser  3/3  0.0s
[-] layout
  layout: 2 tests passed
[+] layout  2/2  0.0s
[+] Testing 0.0s (2/2)
╔════════╤═══════╗
║ Suite  │ Tests ║
╟────────┼───────╢
║ parser │     3 ║
║ layout │     2 ║
╚════════╧═══════╝
- : int = 0
]}

The marks come from the theme, here {!Console.Theme.dos}. Console flushes
standard output before each line it writes, so [check 2>&1 | cat] keeps the
order of the two outputs.

[check --progress=plain] prints these lines on a terminal too. No suite
fails, so [check --progress=quiet] prints none of them, only the table:

{@ocaml[
# Cmdliner.Cmd.eval ~argv:[| "check"; "--progress=quiet" |] cmd;;
╭────────┬───────╮
│ Suite  │ Tests │
├────────┼───────┤
│ parser │     3 │
│ layout │     2 │
╰────────┴───────╯
- : int = 0
]}

[check --progress=json] writes the events for another program to read,
one JSON object to a line ({{!Console_eio.json}JSON events}).

{1:testing Testing the output}

An elapsed time is the one part of the output that depends on the
machine. With [CONSOLE_FROZEN_CLOCK] set to a value other than the empty
string, {!Console_eio.run} reads a clock stopped at zero:

{ul
{- every time reads [0.0s];}
{- no rate shows;}
{- the spinners and the motion stand still.}}

A cram test of [check] then reads the same on every machine. Its [dune]
stanza:

{v
(cram
 (deps %{bin:check}))
v}

and its file, [check.t]:

{v
  $ export CONSOLE_FROZEN_CLOCK=1
  $ check 2>&1
  • parser
    parser: 3 tests passed
  ✓ parser  3/3  0.0s
  • layout
    layout: 2 tests passed
  ✓ layout  2/2  0.0s
  ✓ Testing 0.0s (2/2)
  ╭────────┬───────╮
  │ Suite  │ Tests │
  ├────────┼───────┤
  │ parser │     3 │
  │ layout │     2 │
  ╰────────┴───────╯
v}

The widgets need no clock: [Console.Table.to_string] and the other
[to_string] functions give the text a unit test compares, plain unless
[~styled:true].

